
AI Refactor Prompt Sequence for the Game
Step 0: Initial Instructions (Global context for AI)

You are tasked with refactoring a legacy Java game for cross-platform desktop and mobile. The goal is to:
• Decouple Data, Logic, and UI layers.
• Convert static content (characters, items, quests, world data) from XML + enums into binary format for fast, deterministic access.
• Refactor logic layer to implement snapshot + delta persistence, where: 
• Snapshot = deterministic state (choices made, areas visited, quests completed)
• Delta = dynamic state (player inventory, equipment, temporary stats/buffs)
• The logic layer can read/write only to the persistence system (manual/auto save); all other data layers are read-only.
• Replace JavaFX/WebView UI with LibGDX for cross-platform rendering and input.
• Support autosave (optional periodic flush to cache/temp) and manual save to persistent storage.
• All binary read/write operations use a shared binary engine you design, capable of fast serialization/deserialization.
Refactor incrementally: start with data layer, then logic layer, then UI, then persistence, then optimization, then tests. Provide only one step at a time.
Step 1: Refactor Data Layer

Step 1: Refactor the data layer.
• Extract all static, deterministic game data from the existing XML + enums. This includes: 
• Characters, classes, stats
• Weapons, armor, equipment
• Story branches, quests, flags
• World locations, areas, tiles, NPC states
• Convert data to JSON first for clarity, then serialize into compact binary files.
• Build a binary engine API to read these files efficiently: 
• Random access to individual entries
• Lazy-loading optional for large datasets
• Provide getters only (read-only to logic layer)
• Ensure all internal references use IDs or indices, not object pointers.
Output: a fully refactored data layer with binary files and a clean API that allows logic layer to read static content.
Step 2: Refactor Logic Layer (Snapshot + Delta)

Step 2: Refactor the logic layer.
• Extract all core mechanics (combat, inventory, buffs, movement, quests, events).
• Separate state into: 
• Deterministic state (snapshot): story choices, visited areas, quest progress
• Dynamic state (delta): player inventory, equipment, temporary buffs/stats
• Implement a snapshot engine: 
• Serialize full deterministic state periodically or at checkpoints
• Store to disk/cache as per platform
• Implement a delta engine: 
• Track only changes in memory buffer
• Flush asynchronously to disk/cache at intervals or thresholds
• Expose a clean API to the UI layer: 
• Query state (player, world, inventory)
• Trigger actions (equip, move, interact, use item)
• Use the binary engine from Step 1 for both reading static data and reading/writing snapshots/deltas.
Constraint: The logic layer may read/write only the snapshot/delta persistence system; all other data layers are read-only.
Step 3: Refactor UI Layer

Step 3: Refactor the UI layer.
• Remove all JavaFX/WebView dependencies.
• Implement LibGDX rendering and input.
• Create standardized assets: buttons, menus, effects, animations.
• UI must query logic layer API only for data; it must not modify engine state directly.
• Support platform-specific layout adjustments for desktop vs mobile.
Output: fully functional UI that renders engine state and handles player input.
Step 4: Integrate Manual & Auto Saving

Step 4: Implement manual and auto saving.
• Logic layer writes deterministic snapshots and dynamic deltas to the persistence system only.
• Autosave: periodically flush snapshots/deltas to cache (mobile) or temp folder (desktop) asynchronously.
• Manual save: flush full snapshot + delta to permanent storage (disk).
• On load: reconstruct engine state from last snapshot + deltas.
• Ensure binary engine is used for all read/write operations.
• RAM buffer holds current session; cache/temp is optional for crash recovery; permanent save is authoritative.
Step 5: Optimization

Step 5: Optimize performance for desktop and mobile.
• Minimize in-memory footprint; keep only active chunks and recent deltas.
• Batch delta writes to reduce I/O overhead.
• Lazy-load large datasets from binary files.
• Use asynchronous writes for snapshots/deltas.
• Avoid runtime XML/JSON parsing; everything should load from binaries.
• Test that frame rate remains smooth and memory usage is reasonable on mobile devices.
Step 6: Testing & Validation

Step 6: Implement comprehensive testing.
• Unit tests for snapshot + delta reconstruction (engine state matches expected outcomes).
• Validate UI correctly reflects engine state.
• Test manual save, autosave, and load functionality on both mobile and desktop.
• Ensure deterministic state remains consistent and dynamic state correctly updates.
• Optional: generate logs to verify binary engine read/write correctness.
Notes for AI Execution
• Each step builds on the previous; do not merge steps.
• Always use binary engine for read/write.
• Logic layer persistence is the only writable interface; all static data remains read-only.
• Autosave is optional, manual save is authoritative.
• Keep UI fully decoupled from logic and data layers.